From dc1acf498d347b4014c75374ce2981777dfce062 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 22:51:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Count item 4-5's legacy frame, and clear two instrument
 defects 0103 flagged
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

0109 bounded the Annex B caller/arguments frame at <=1.65% of the corpus by
charging it to every call with a JavaScript callee, because nothing said how many
of those callees actually carry the pair. Counted at the entry it is 35 715 923
of 59 372 494 calls — 60.16% of all calls and 88.14% of the ones with a
JavaScript callee — worth 1 586 ms of a 108 879 ms driver: 1.46%. The ceiling was
tight and the item is real.

Richards pays it on 100% of its calls, DeltaBlue 96%, Typescript 75%, Box2D 70%,
EarleyBoyer 51% — and PdfJS 0.59%, Gameboy 0.02%. The two that escape are the two
that are strict, which is the same split the strict-transition census found from
the other side: strict functions carry no legacy pair, so a program written in
strict mode does not pay this at all.

The counter also settles a confound in 0109's attribution rather than leaving it
inferred. An arrow differs from an ordinary sloppy function in more than the
legacy frame if it also skips the sloppy `this` coercion, so the probe now asks
the engine which of the two each shape performs: arrow and method both coerce,
and neither pushes a frame, so they differ in exactly one thing and the control
was sound. On the corpus thisCoercions equals legacyFrames to the call, so a
single "ordinary sloppy function" predicate gates both.

Two instrument defects 0103 surfaced and left, now closed.

The property-map policy table labelled round-up-16 "current: VirtualMemory.Allocate
as written", false since 2-7 shipped geometric-from-NodeBlock — so the simulation
named a baseline nobody runs and left the policy the engine actually uses
unmarked. Both labels corrected against the code.

And one histogram bucket read -15. The cause is structural: the table counts each
map once by moving it out of the previous bucket as it grows, which assumes its
arrival there was counted, and a map already in that bucket when Reset zeroed the
table has a decrement with nothing to cancel. A negative count is not a
distribution and every share is divided by a total containing it. The decrement
now clamps at zero and the clamps are counted: 17 this run, 0 negatives, so the
defect reproduces and the fix is demonstrated rather than assumed.

Compiler suite green at 1 267, storage suite at 46.
---
